// Book One, Chapter 3 (session 8). Rome by night, and the house that takes you in.
// A city of wheels and heartbeats, a friend on the rooftops, and a woman with an abacus.
@chapter b1c03
@title The Merchant's House
@book burning-city
@version 1

# start
## Rome
$ do phase("night", 3)
$ _fed = 0
$ _tiro = 0
$ _roof = 0
You walk for three nights, because the days are no friend of yours, and you come to Rome on the fourth with the stars thinning at the edges and the road gone to noise.
You hear it before you see it. It is not the noise of a village, which is made of separate sounds, a dog and a door and a voice. It is a single enormous sound, like the sea heard from inside a shell, and when you stand still at the last milestone you understand that it is made of people. Hundreds of thousands of them. Every one has a pulse, and every pulse is a small bright thread, and together they make a weave so thick that you cannot find the edge of it.
[roman] You were born to this noise, or to a quieter part of it, and it is strange to come back to it a stranger.
[gaul briton] You have heard Rome described by men who hated it, and by men who feared it, and by men who had only ever sold it grain. None of them told you how loud it would be.
[north_african egyptian] You have seen great cities. You have never seen one that seemed to be holding its breath and shouting at the same time.
[judean parthian silk_road] You were told that all roads end here. Standing at the end of one, you are not at all sure that this is a recommendation.
The guards at the gate are bored and half asleep, and they wave you through with the rest of the night's traffic. It is the law, you remember, that wheeled carts may not use the streets by day. So Rome saves its wagons for the dark. They come in a long creaking river, loaded with stone and wine and timber and live pigs, and the drivers curse each other in six languages, and the whole city shakes on its foundations.
It is the loveliest thing you have ever seen. It is also the loneliest.
?if band("injury") != "whole"
  You carry the fire with you. {cap(voice("injury"))}, and the cloak hides the worst of it, and nobody looks at you twice, which is the great mercy of a big city.
?end
{cap(voice("hunger"))}. A hundred thousand heartbeats go past you in the dark, and every one of them is a door you could open.
-> city

# city
## The first night
:city
?if actions_left() == 3
  Dawn is some hours off. You stand in the shadow of an arch with the cloak held close, and you have no idea where you are going to spend the day.
?elif actions_left() == 2
  The night is half spent. The carts are thinner now, and the wine shops are putting up their shutters.
?else
  There is a paleness at the bottom of the sky, behind the hills, that you do not like at all.
?end
